::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L06_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.1
Question:
Evaluate 2³.
{
=A. 8#2³ \= 2 times 2 times 2 \= 8.
~B. 6#This multiplies 2 by 3. An exponent means repeated multiplication.
~C. 9#This squares 3 instead of using 2 as the base.
~D. 5#This adds the base and exponent. Exponents do not mean addition.
}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L06_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.1
Question:
Evaluate 5⁰.
{
~A. 0#This is a common misconception. A zero exponent does not make the value zero.
~B. 5#This keeps the base unchanged. A zero exponent changes the value to 1 for nonzero bases.
=C. 1#Any nonzero base raised to the zero power equals 1.
~D. 10#This adds 5 and 0 or doubles the base. Use the zero exponent property.
}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L06_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.1
Question:
Evaluate 3² times 3¹.
{
~A. 9#This stops after evaluating 3². The expression also includes 3¹.
=B. 27#3² times 3¹ \= 3³ \= 27.
~C. 18#This multiplies 3² by 2 instead of by 3¹.
~D. 81#This adds too much to the exponent. The exponent should be 2 + 1 \= 3.
}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L06_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.1
Question:
Evaluate 4³ / 4¹.
{
~A. 4#This subtracts exponents correctly but stops at 4¹. The result should be 4².
~B. 12#This divides the exponent 3 by 1 or combines numbers incorrectly. Use quotient of powers.
~C. 64#This evaluates only 4³ and ignores the division.
=D. 16#4³ / 4¹ \= 4² \= 16.
}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L06_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.1
Question:
Evaluate 16⁽¹⁄²⁾.
{
=A. 4#16⁽¹⁄²⁾ \= sqrt(16) \= 4.
~B. 8#This divides 16 by 2. An exponent of 1/2 means square root.
~C. 32#This multiplies by 2 instead of finding the square root.
~D. 256#This squares 16 instead of finding its square root.
}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L06_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.1
Question:
Evaluate 27⁽¹⁄³⁾.
{
~A. 9#This divides 27 by 3. An exponent of 1/3 means cube root.
=B. 3#27⁽¹⁄³⁾ \= cuberoot(27) \= 3.
~C. 81#This multiplies by 3 instead of taking the cube root.
~D. 729#This cubes 9 or misuses the exponent. The rational exponent asks for a root.
}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L06_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.1
Question:
Evaluate 25⁽³⁄²⁾.
{
~A. 15#This multiplies 25 by 3/2. A rational exponent represents a root and a power.
~B. 75#This multiplies 25 by 3. Use the square root first.
~C. 625#This squares 25. The exponent is not 2.
=D. 125#25⁽³⁄²⁾ \= (sqrt(25))³ \= 5³ \= 125.
}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L06_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.1
Question:
Evaluate 10⁻².
{
~A. -100#A negative exponent does not make the value negative. It creates a reciprocal.
~B. -20#This multiplies 10 by -2. Exponents do not mean multiplication by the exponent.
~C. 100#This ignores the negative exponent. The reciprocal is needed.
=D. 1/100#10⁻² \= 1/10² \= 1/100.
}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L06_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.1
Question:
Evaluate 2³ times 2².
{
=A. 32#2³ times 2² \= 2⁵ \= 32.
~B. 12#This multiplies visible exponents or factors incorrectly. Use product of powers.
~C. 64#This multiplies exponents to get 2⁶. Add the exponents instead.
~D. 10#This adds 8 and 2 or combines values incorrectly.
}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L06_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.1
Question:
Evaluate (3²)².
{
~A. 12#This multiplies the base and exponents incorrectly. Evaluate the power of a power.
~B. 18#This doubles 9 but does not square it.
~C. 729#This uses 3⁶. The exponent should be 2 times 2 \= 4.
=D. 81#(3²)² \= 3⁴ \= 81.
}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L06_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.1
Question:
Evaluate 64⁽²⁄³⁾.
{
~A. 8#This finds cuberoot(64) but does not square the result.
=B. 16#64⁽²⁄³⁾ \= (cuberoot(64))² \= 4² \= 16.
~C. 32#This divides or multiplies by parts of the exponent. Use cube root then square.
~D. 128#This multiplies 64 by 2. Rational exponents represent roots and powers.
}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L06_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.1
Question:
Evaluate 7⁰.
{
~A. 0#This is the common zero exponent misconception. The value is not 0.
~B. 7#This keeps the base unchanged. A zero exponent gives 1 for nonzero bases.
=C. 1#Any nonzero base raised to the zero power equals 1.
~D. 70#This treats the exponent as a digit. Exponents describe powers, not place value.
}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L06_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.2
Question:
Rewrite x² times x⁶.
{
=A. x⁸#The bases match, so add the exponents: 2 + 6 \= 8.
~B. x¹²#This multiplies the exponents. Product of powers uses addition.
~C. 2x⁸#This adds an extra coefficient that is not in the original expression.
~D. x⁴#This subtracts the exponents instead of adding them.
}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L06_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.2
Question:
Rewrite m⁹ / m⁴.
{
~A. m¹³#This adds exponents. Quotient of powers uses subtraction.
~B. m³⁶#This multiplies exponents. Use subtraction for division with matching bases.
=C. m⁵#m⁹ / m⁴ \= m⁹-4 \= m⁵.
~D. m⁴#This subtracts in the wrong order or drops too many factors.
}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L06_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.2
Question:
Rewrite (p⁴)³.
{
~A. p⁷#This adds the exponents. Power of a power uses multiplication.
~B. p¹#This subtracts the exponents. Subtraction is for quotients with matching bases.
~C. p⁶⁴#This raises 4 to the third power. Multiply the exponents instead.
=D. p¹²#(p⁴)³ \= p⁴ times 3 \= p¹².
}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L06_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.2
Question:
Rewrite y⁻² with a positive exponent.
{
~A. -y²#A negative exponent does not make the term negative. It creates a reciprocal.
=B. 1/y²#y⁻² \= 1/y².
~C. y²#This changes the exponent sign without moving the factor to the denominator.
~D. y/2#This treats the exponent like division by 2. Use y² in the denominator.
}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L06_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.2
Question:
Rewrite a⁰, assuming a is not 0.
{
=A. 1#a⁰ \= 1 for a not equal to 0.
~B. 0#This is the common zero exponent mistake. A nonzero base to the zero power equals 1.
~C. a#This keeps the base unchanged. The zero exponent property gives 1.
~D. 1/a#This is related to a negative exponent, not a zero exponent.
}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L06_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.2
Question:
Rewrite (2x)³.
{
~A. 2x³#This applies the exponent only to x. The grouping symbols include both 2 and x.
=B. 8x³#(2x)³ \= 2³ˣ³ \= 8x³.
~C. 6x³#This multiplies 2 by 3 instead of cubing 2.
~D. 8x#This cubes 2 but does not cube x.
}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L06_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.2
Question:
Rewrite (3x²)².
{
~A. 6x⁴#This multiplies 3 by 2 instead of squaring 3.
~B. 9x²#This squares 3 but does not apply the outside exponent to x².
=C. 9x⁴#(3x²)² \= 3²ˣ⁴ \= 9x⁴.
~D. 3x⁴#This applies the exponent to x² but not to the coefficient 3.
}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L06_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.2
Question:
Rewrite x⁵ times x⁻².
{
~A. x⁷#This adds 5 and 2 but ignores the negative sign.
~B. x⁻¹⁰#This multiplies the exponents. Product of powers uses addition.
=C. x³#Add the exponents: 5 + (-2) \= 3.
~D. 1/x⁷#This treats the whole result as negative. The exponent sum is positive.
}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L06_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.1
Question:
Rewrite 2³ x 2⁴ using one exponent.
{
~A. 2¹²#This multiplies the exponents instead of adding them.
=B. 2⁷#When multiplying powers with the same base, add the exponents: 3 + 4 \= 7.
~C. 4⁷#The base should remain 2 because both powers have base 2.
~D. 2¹#This subtracts the exponents instead of adding them.
}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L06_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.1
Question:
Simplify (x²)³.
{
~A. x⁵#This adds the exponents, but power of a power requires multiplication.
~B. x⁸#This does not match the exponent rule for a power raised to a power.
~C. x¹#This subtracts the exponents, which is not the correct rule here.
=D. x⁶#For a power raised to a power, multiply the exponents: 2 x 3 \= 6.
}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L06_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.1
Question:
What is the value of 5⁰?
{
~A. 0#A zero exponent does not make the value zero when the base is nonzero.
~B. 5#This would be 5¹, not 5⁰.
=C. 1#Any nonzero number raised to the zero power equals 1.
~D. 25#This would be 5², not 5⁰.
}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L06_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.2
Question:
Which expression is equivalent to y⁻³ using positive exponents?
{
=A. 1/y³#A negative exponent moves the power to the denominator: y⁻³ \= 1/y³.
~B. y³#This changes the sign of the exponent without moving the factor to the denominator.
~C. -y³#A negative exponent does not make the expression negative.
~D. 1/y⁻³#This still contains a negative exponent, so it is not written using positive exponents.
}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L06_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.2
Question:
What is the value of 16⁽¹⁄²⁾?
{
=A. 4#An exponent of 1/2 means the square root, and the square root of 16 is 4.
~B. 8#This divides 16 by 2 instead of taking the square root.
~C. 32#This multiplies 16 by 2 instead of taking the square root.
~D. 256#This squares 16 instead of taking the square root.
}
